@flexiarg baldwin/assimilable-traits-need-heritable-shadows
@title An Assimilable Plastic Trait Needs a Heritable Shadow
@sigils [🌀/刀]
@keywords genotype-phenotype-map, expression, hold, fixation, mutation, metaca
@audience MetaCA designers, evolutionary-computation researchers
@tone mechanistic
@style pattern
@childof baldwin/mechanism
@references [baldwin/assimilation-requires-a-resolved-path baldwin/charge-for-realized-work-not-for-capacity exotic/hybrid-execution-semantics]

! conclusion:
  A plastic contribution is assimilable only when an inherited alternative for the
  same functional contribution is expressed enough for selection to prepare it before
  plasticity is removed. A dormant random allele revealed only by a hold mutation is
  not such an alternative.

  + context:
    A MetaCA plastic locus rewrites its rule from contextual phenotype information. A
    held locus instead retains its inherited initial rule. In the tested genome, the
    inherited rule can remain phenotypically hidden while rewriting is active; flipping
    `hold` therefore both removes the learned mechanism and exposes an unprepared byte.

  + IF:
    The experiment proposes that plastic-to-held mutations will assimilate a useful
    within-lifetime rewrite.

  + HOWEVER:
    The learned terminal rule may depend on time and context rather than correspond to
    one fixed byte. Independent rule and hold mutations require a coordinated event;
    coupling hold to a uniformly random byte raises proposal supply but does not copy
    the functional relationship learned during life.

  + THEN:
    Represent an inherited shadow that affects phenotype before full fixation. Options
    include a default rule mixed with the rewritten rule by per-locus penetrance, a
    heritable initialization that changes learning speed, or a lower-dimensional
    developmental bias shared across contexts. Test whether shadow variants are
    selected while plasticity is still present, then reduce their plastic complement.

  + BECAUSE:
    Selection can refine only expressed inherited variation. Hinton and Nowlan's fixed
    allele is immediately expressed and a correct replacement reduces the remaining
    search dimension. In contrast, holding an arbitrary MetaCA initial byte is an
    ablation plus random replacement, so selection against holding is expected even
    when a different representation could assimilate the function.

    + EXPRESSION-IS-NOT-SELECTABILITY:
      A shadow variable is necessary and not sufficient, and the MetaCA case shows why.
      The inherited field *is* expressed — it is the initial condition of the rewriting
      — yet it is still not selectable, because sixty steps of rewriting wash out its
      contribution to the scored outcome and the cost term reads only the plasticity
      machinery. So there are two distinct gates: the inherited alternative must affect
      the phenotype, and some fitness component must differ between genomes that differ
      only in that alternative. Adding a latent allele to a fitness function that is
      flat in genome content would leave it as unprepared as the byte it replaces. See
      `baldwin/charge-for-realized-work-not-for-capacity` for the second gate.

    + COUNTERFACTUAL:
      If prepared shadow alleles remain selectable when partially expressed but random
      held bytes do not, the previous negative diagnoses genotype-phenotype mismatch,
      not load-bearing plasticity in principle.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Formalize expression-before-fixation as an ExperimentalDesign obligation.
    - Compare random coupled replacement with prepared-shadow mutation without direct inheritance of learned states.
    - Pair any latent-allele proposal with a fitness component that reads inherited content; neither gate passes alone.
